\image(figures/gev.svg)
// result_end

At Port Pirie the shape estimate is \(-0.05\) with a standard error of 0.10, and the likelihood-ratio test cannot distinguish the GEV from the Gumbel (p = 0.62). AIC prefers the simpler model. L-moments give almost the same parameters. Fremantle is different. Its shape is clearly negative (\(-0.22\), SE 0.064; LR p = 0.003), so sea levels there have an estimated upper bound of about 2.13 m. Forcing a Gumbel fit there overstates the 1000-year level by 0.44 m, and its interval [2.29, 2.61] lies entirely above the GEV interval [1.90, 2.22].

The return-level table shows why profile-likelihood intervals are preferred for long periods. The likelihood for a high quantile is skewed: values far above the estimate remain plausible, values far below do not. For the Port Pirie 1000-year level, the symmetric Wald interval [4.38, 5.69] has a lower limit below the 100-year estimate, while the profile interval [4.66, 6.47] extends much further upward. Under the Gumbel model the same interval is only [4.97, 5.53], because a fixed \(\xi=0\) removes the dominant source of uncertainty at long return periods. Preferring the Gumbel model because a test cannot reject it gives intervals that are too narrow.

The simulation confirms this. With a true shape of 0.1, the profile interval for the 100-year level covers 93% of the time from 30 years of data and 96% from 60, while the Wald interval covers only 86–88%. Both estimators of \(\xi\) are slightly biased toward zero, with L-moments more so at n = 30. Those biases are small next to the Gumbel model's: it puts the median 100-year level near 4.8 instead of 5.84, and its interval covers the truth only 76% of the time with 30 years of data, and 58% with 60. More data do not rescue a misspecified tail.

>> Practical guidance

- Fit the GEV and report \(\hat\xi\) with its uncertainty. Use the Gumbel model only with strong external reasons, since a nonsignificant LR test is weak evidence when n is a few decades.
- Report long return levels with profile-likelihood intervals, not Wald or delta-method intervals.
- Use L-moments as starting values and as a check, and as the estimator for very short records (n < 25), where they are often more stable than ML. The ML estimate behaves poorly when \(\xi<-0.5\), where the support boundary dominates.
- Block maxima use only one value per block. When data are plentiful, threshold exceedances (generalized Pareto) use more information. Check stationarity: trends in the series call for covariates in \(\mu\) or \(\sigma\).

>> References

- S. Coles (2001). *An Introduction to Statistical Modeling of Extreme Values*, ch. 3. Springer. doi:10.1007/978-1-4471-3675-0
- A. F. Jenkinson (1955). The frequency distribution of the annual maximum (or minimum) values of meteorological elements. *Quarterly Journal of the Royal Meteorological Society* 81, 158-171. doi:10.1002/qj.49708134804
- J. R. M. Hosking, J. R. Wallis and E. F. Wood (1985). Estimation of the generalized extreme-value distribution by the method of probability-weighted moments. *Technometrics* 27(3), 251-261. doi:10.1080/00401706.1985.10488049
- E. Gilleland and R. W. Katz (2016). extRemes 2.0: An extreme value analysis package in R. *Journal of Statistical Software* 72(8), 1-39. doi:10.18637/jss.v072.i08
